"Hold your hand out of a window on a breezy day. Something pushes against it, but you cannot see anything there. That something is air. Air is real stuff, and when it moves from one place to another, we call it wind. What makes the air move? The answer starts with the sun. Sunshine does not heat every place the same. Land warms up quickly, and it cools down quickly too. Water is slower. It takes a long time to warm up and a long time to cool down. Think about a beach on a sunny day. By the afternoon, the land is warmer than the sea. The air over the land warms up, gets lighter, and rises. Cooler air from over the sea moves in along the ground to take its place. That is a sea breeze. It usually begins at about ten or eleven in the morning and is strongest at about two or three in the afternoon. After the sun goes down, the land cools quickly, but the sea stays almost as warm as before. Now the land is the cooler side, so the air near the ground flows the other way, from the land out to the sea. That is a land breeze. It is weaker than the sea breeze. The same thing happens on a much bigger scale across a whole year. In winter, the great continent of Asia becomes much colder than the ocean, and cold, dry air flows out from the land. In summer, the continent becomes much hotter than the ocean, and moist air flows in from the sea. A wind that changes direction with the seasons is called a monsoon. Taiwan lies inside one of the strongest monsoon regions in the world. People name a wind for the direction it comes from. A north wind comes from the north, and a sea breeze comes from the sea. We measure its speed in meters a second, or we give it a number from 0 to 12 on a scale that sailors began to use more than two hundred years ago. So the next time the wind pushes your hand, you will know what is happening: somewhere, air is rising, and this air is on its way to take its place.": "09150d70a56d",
